      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "da8a58342c11c3a369904371ad7028da820ba943cac0116432031e0d3aef59a8"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Galton (1879) first pointed out a possible explanation for the commonness of the log-normal distribution. Suppose the phenotype can be represented as a product of a large number of independent factors $ z = x_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3 \\cdots x_n $. Upon logarithmic transformation, $ y = \\ln z = \\ln x_1 + \\ln x_2 + \\ln x_3 + \\cdots + \\ln x_n $, and under the central limit theorem, $ \\ln z $ will tend to normality as $ n $ becomes large. Thus, a simple explanation for a log-normal distribution is the existence of multiplicative interaction between a large number of factors.",
      "source_page": 309,
      "printed_page": 289,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter11:p003:b8"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        67.0,
        639.0,
        1029.0,
        885.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        309
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          67.0,
          639.0,
          1029.0,
          885.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "a0613bb1e2d6663d737ff640ed87d25fd1ba159e12c8f54dc3bf7ab4d97ccac9"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "A number of other scale transformations, such as power functions and trigonometric functions, are available if the original data depart from both normality and log-normality (Wright 1968). If, for example, length measures in a population are known to be normally distributed, then it is likely that a cube root transformation would be required to normalize the distribution of weights, since weight is usually proportional to the cube of the length. The Box-Cox transformation,",
      "source_page": 309,
      "printed_page": 289,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter11:p003:b9"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        61.0,
